  "readmeFile": "ReadmeNuget.md",
  "releaseNotes": "# Breaking Changes (Scripting)\n      - `*=` now performs multiplication (was incorrectly computing addition). `x = 3; x *= 5;` now yields 15 (was 8).\n      - Postfix `x++` / `x--` now returns the OLD value when used as an expression (previously returned null). Prefix `++x` / `--x` returns the NEW value. Statements like `x++;` are unaffected.\n      - Reassignment to explicitly-typed local variables (e.g. `int x; x = ...;`) now goes through NetObjectConverter, preserving the declared type. Values that cannot be converted become null. `var`-declared variables are unchanged.\n      - Reassignment to explicitly-typed module-level variables follows the same rule, including cross-module assignment (`mod.x = value;`).\n      - Script function overload resolution now uses best-match scoring (previously selected the first applicable declaration). Exact-type matches win over implicit conversions.\n      - Defining multiple script functions with identical signatures now throws a runtime error (\"Duplicate function definition\"). Previously, the first definition was silently used.\n      - Identifier resolution order changed: module-declared members (Field / Layout / ScriptVariables / CurrentUser) are now resolved BEFORE services and static-providing types (`Math`, `DateTime`, `Logger`, etc.). Field names that collide with these reserved type names will now resolve to the Field rather than the type.\n      - Method overload resolution on .NET members now accounts for inheritance distance (BaseType chain) when multiple candidates share the same `IsAssignableFrom` score, preferring the most-derived parameter type.\n\n      # Enhancements (Scripting)\n      - Added bitwise operators `^`, `<<`, `>>`, and unary `~`.\n      - Added unary `+` operator.\n      - `++` / `--` now work on array elements (e.g. `ary[0]++;`).\n      - Comprehensive test coverage added for operator boundaries, overload resolution, type conversions, name conflicts, and literal parsing.",
